{"cluster_id": "C-0903", "topic": "Full-catalog token equality is load-bearing because", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B049-055"], "canonical_claim": "Full-catalog token equality is load-bearing because comparing only the row value would accept a stale snapshot whose value happens to match the fresh snapshot.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task-5-prefold-cut-provenance-report.md#ownership-rationale"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0904", "topic": "`snapshotOf` rejects every non-`Live` state with", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-007", "B050-017"], "canonical_claim": "`snapshotOf` rejects every non-`Live` state with `CORRUPTED_DATA` before constructing a snapshot DTO. Additionally, `snapshotOf` refuses every non-live state with `CORRUPTED_DATA` before DTO construction, and terminal replay tests preserve `Removed` plus its exact removal id.", "issue_ids": ["T5"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-brief.md#L13-L13", ".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-report.md#L28-L29"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0905", "topic": "Writer recovery treats absent `ckpt.lifeepoch` as", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B030-031", "B050-034"], "canonical_claim": "Writer recovery treats absent `_ckpt.life_epoch` as corruption for `Live` and `Removing`; it does not use the smallest listed log id as genesis, and `Creating` or catalog-absent lives are not recovered.; Recovery must not fall back to `hint_log_ids.front()` or fabricate `life_epoch` with `value_or`.", "issue_ids": ["T5b"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5b-list-audit.md#L18-L19", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-29-cas-stage-b-namespace-life-amendments.md#make-recovery-fully-list-independent"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0906", "topic": "The inclusive `committedthrough` frontier is the", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-035"], "canonical_claim": "The inclusive `committed_through` frontier is the authoritative missing-slot witness and cannot be extended or shortened by listed ids.", "issue_ids": ["T5b"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5b-list-audit.md#L19-L20"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0907", "topic": "A selected listed object disappearing no", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-038"], "canonical_claim": "A selected listed object disappearing no longer causes a fresh full-list restart; exact checkpoint re-reads resolve ambiguous observations and recovery fails closed when authority still requires a missing object.", "issue_ids": ["T5b"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5b-list-audit.md#L34-L36"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0908", "topic": "The modeled `ForgetTerminal` invariant requires completed", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-071"], "canonical_claim": "The modeled `ForgetTerminal` invariant requires completed FORGET to have a latched fence and a `Vanished` state; removing trip 2 violates it because an in-flight reclaim can restore `Live` and `mayMutate() == TRUE`.", "issue_ids": ["I1", "T15"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/tla-rev7-report.md#L38-L49"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0909", "topic": "The modeled `EarnedFarewell` invariant permits farewell", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-073"], "canonical_claim": "The modeled `EarnedFarewell` invariant permits farewell only after a provable drain, and `OneWay` forbids transitions from `IdentityLost` or `Vanished` back to `Live` or `Transient`.", "issue_ids": ["I2", "I3", "T15"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/tla-rev7-report.md#L55-L61"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0910", "topic": "`LocalObjectStorage::listObjects` uses an explicit-stack non-recursive walk", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B051-067"], "canonical_claim": "`LocalObjectStorage::listObjects` uses an explicit-stack non-recursive walk with `error_code` overloads and a symlink guard to emulate object-store snapshot behavior while a sibling detached ref directory is removed.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#local-object-storage-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0911", "topic": "The one writer-side path that fails", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-022"], "canonical_claim": "The one writer-side path that fails closed on corruption is a detected logical-hash collision: in store_blob_safely, when a writer reuses an existing logical-hash key it verifies the existing object's header hashes to the expected logical hash (verify(existing_hdr.logical_hash == H)); if it does not, the system quarantines the key and raises a hard error rather than silently papering over the mismatch, since silent reuse of a mis-verifying object would let corrupt or colliding content be served under a live ref.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#hash-collision-quarantine"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0912", "topic": "Key invariants: INV-OVER-COUNT-ONLY -- every failure", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-025"], "canonical_claim": "Key invariants: INV-OVER-COUNT-ONLY -- every failure (lost fold, crashed leader, stale snap, duplicated records) delays reclamation but none accelerates a delete past the gates. INV-S3-COMPLETE -- S3 alone is both the durable truth and sufficient coordination; Keeper is an accelerator and total Keeper loss loses nothing and blocks nothing. INC-MONO -- for a fixed (ns, shard), every successive materialization of the shard object carries a strictly greater incarnation, closing ABA by construction. WriterEpochMonotoneUnique -- the epoch-object allocator never reissues a writer_epoch for a server_root_id, across crash/restart and across mount deletion.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#key-invariants"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0913", "topic": "Backend safety depends on exact-token conditional", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-027"], "canonical_claim": "Backend safety depends on exact-token conditional delete: a DELETE If-Match token with a wrong token MUST fail, not silently succeed; this is probed at startup and the disk fails closed if the backend does not enforce it. Bucket versioning must never be enabled on the CA prefix; versioning-suspended buckets are also rejected because a delete inserts a null delete marker, breaking the one-live-object model. A startup probe verifies this functionally.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#backend-contract"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0914", "topic": "`gtestcasholeylistdetector.cpp`'s two kept tests (`OmittedRemoveRecordIsSkippedForever`, `OmittedActivationNeverPermitsDeletingALiveBlob`)", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-088", "B047-004"], "canonical_claim": "`gtest_cas_holey_list_detector.cpp`'s two kept tests (`OmittedRemoveRecordIsSkippedForever`, `OmittedActivationNeverPermitsDeletingALiveBlob`) prove the arithmetic ref-intake's resilience to a holey LIST page independently of probe A, a class of coverage unrelated to and unaffected by probe A's removal.; The two holey-LIST tests `OmittedRemoveRecordIsSkippedForever` and `OmittedActivationNeverPermitsDeletingALiveBlob` are retained because they cover skipped-transaction retention-leak and data-loss behavior independently of probe A.", "issue_ids": ["T5"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t5-draft-report.md#L97-L124", ".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t5-report.md#L72-L78"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0915", "topic": "`CaGcShardIncarnationCore.tla` checks `INVNODANGLING` and `INVNOORPHANEDGE` across", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-026", "AUDIT-M2-004"], "canonical_claim": "`CaGcShardIncarnationCore.tla` checks `INV_NO_DANGLING` and `INV_NO_ORPHAN_EDGE` across 724,944 distinct states and proves that two orthogonal coordinates (a durable never-reused `incarnation` stamped at shard creation, and the pool-global GC `round`) are both necessary; three negative controls (`SabotageNewbornNoFloor`, `SabotagePathKeyedCursor`, `SabotageDeleteBeforeFold`) each show a counterexample when one coordinate is removed.; The core invariants (INV-NO-LOSS, INV-NO-DANGLE, INV-NO-RETURN, INV-JOURNAL-COVERAGE, MonotoneGC) are model-checked by CaIncarnationCore.tla; mount-safety invariants (WriterEpochMonotoneUnique, SupersededWriterMakesNoMutation) are model-checked by a focused Phase-0 TLA+ model; INV-NO-DANGLING and INV-NO-ORPHAN-EDGE under D1 are model-checked by CaGcShardIncarnationCore.tla (724,944 states, GREEN as of 2026-07-01).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#key-invariants", "docs/superpowers/cas/02-methodology.md#L184-L197"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0916", "topic": "Promote's publish-gate obligations (MR-1/MR-2/MR-3) apply only", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-017"], "canonical_claim": "Promote's publish-gate obligations (MR-1/MR-2/MR-3) apply only to the non-tokened evidence/copy-forward dependency subset since EDGE-BEFORE-OBSERVE: MR-1 consults durable deleted-token history, not only a cached retired set; MR-2/F1 re-validates the dependency's current physical state (still present, still carrying the originally-observed token, not merely 'not condemned when first seen'); MR-3/F2 requires bottom-up tree publish so a manifest may publish only when every direct child is present and non-condemned at publish time. A tokened dependency gets zero promote-time re-validation against any of these.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L349-L374"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0917", "topic": "`INV-2` (precommit-first / reachability-before-content): the build-root", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-018"], "canonical_claim": "`INV-2` (precommit-first / reachability-before-content): the build-root precommit must be published before any pool content GET/HEAD/PUT of the build's content; the full write ordering stages blobs into local temp files, then makes the edge-bearing manifest body durable via `stageManifest`, then lands the owner event via `precommitAdd`, and only then do blob uploads reach the pool. A writer dying mid-upload therefore leaves blobs whose edges already exist in a folded body, so there is no structural 'orphan blob' class \u2014 an object in `blobs/` with no edge-bearing body in history should be impossible and fsck may treat one as an anomaly.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L501-L510"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0918", "topic": "The CAS backend contract's `TOKEN \u27f9", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-076"], "canonical_claim": "The CAS backend contract's `TOKEN \u27f9 CONTENT` requirement (a repeated token implies unchanged bytes) is the one item that cannot be probed cheaply and is therefore a documented, unverified assumption on every backend, not enforced by the capability probe.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#backend-requirements"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0919", "topic": "CAS's transient-vs-terminal exception classifier separates a", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-161"], "canonical_claim": "CAS's transient-vs-terminal exception classifier separates a READ-plane and a WRITE-plane per an audit of every throw site tree-wide: the READ plane is the only one with destructive reach and had exactly two sites, of 106 GC-ref throw sites tree-wide 27 were transient (19 `NETWORK_ERROR`, 5 `INVALID_STATE`, 3 `ABORTED`); a write-once `ABORTED`-on-write-once site (718 occurrences) was judged a real defect while the deposition/fence family was deliberately left `INVALID_STATE` because recoding it to `ABORTED` would regress by escaping a backoff exemption.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L2155-L2157"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0920", "topic": "CAS's single true read-side correctness hole", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-179"], "canonical_claim": "CAS's single true read-side correctness hole (labeled X1/R1, \"missing read\"): a ref-less/cross-node reader resolves ref to manifest to blob-location at plan time, discards the manifest, then performs a deferred, unpinned blob GET later; if the ref is dropped and GC completes condemn to pending to delete before that GET fires, a committed datum becomes unreadable mid-query (`NoSuchKey`). This is bounded to the write/GC plane being otherwise loss-free; the fix direction is an ephemeral reader pin folded into the GC mark/ack-floor union, or bounding query lifetime below the condemn-to-delete latency.", "issue_ids": ["R1", "X1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L618-L618", "docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L753-L753", "docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L822-L825"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0921", "topic": "CAS's security perimeter is exactly the", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-191"], "canonical_claim": "CAS's security perimeter is exactly the S3 bucket credential: there is no intra-pool authorization, identities are self-asserted and unsigned, and blobs are shared pool-wide by content hash with no namespace in the blob key. Combined with non-cryptographic CityHash128 content-addressing and reads that never re-verify payload-against-key, this enables SEC-1 (High) collision poisoning: because dedup is HEAD-first and blobs are pool-global, an attacker who can craft a `CityHash128` collision can pre-seed a poisoned blob under the victim's future hash so a victim in a different table/tenant later writing the benign content dedups to the poison and reads attacker bytes, with all CRC checks passing (latent in a single-trust-domain pool, real the moment a pool is multi-tenant).", "issue_ids": ["SEC-1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L833-L845"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0922", "topic": "SEC-8: provenance (`serverid`, timestamp, op, `buildid`)", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-198"], "canonical_claim": "SEC-8: provenance (`server_id`, timestamp, op, `build_id`) and the `CasEvent` sink give a useful audit trail, but everything is self-asserted and forgeable by a pool-write adversary, so it is good for debugging and honest-fault forensics but not an integrity/attribution control against a malicious insider.", "issue_ids": ["SEC-8"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1030-L1034"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0923", "topic": "SEC-9: blobs are plaintext table bytes", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-199"], "canonical_claim": "SEC-9: blobs are plaintext table bytes; confidentiality relies entirely on S3 SSE + bucket policy + TLS, and content-addressing also makes content equality externally observable (same content maps to the same key), which deployments with confidential data across tenants in one pool should assume as an equality-leakage risk.", "issue_ids": ["SEC-9"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1036-L1041"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0924", "topic": "The full set of code sites", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-208"], "canonical_claim": "The full set of code sites sharing the renameParts -> Keeper-decision -> parts-commit slot audited for the part-durability-before-Keeper-commit fix is: `ReplicatedMergeTreeSink::commitPart` (INSERT, the only dedup-token site and hence the only silent-loss path), `renameMergedTemporaryPart` (merges), `MutateFromLogEntryTask::finalize`, `fetchPart`, `executeLogEntry`, `executeReplaceRange`, `replacePartitionFromImpl`, `movePartitionToTable`, `renameAndCommitEmptyParts`, and `createEmptyPartInsteadOfLost`; non-INSERT paths hit the same window but only cause replicated-log-entry re-execution churn, not acknowledged data loss.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-dataloss-traced-root-cause.md#L112"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0925", "topic": "The ref-chain complete-cut design's generation-change safety", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-305", "B040-029"], "canonical_claim": "The ref-chain complete-cut design's generation-change safety was confirmed to have no durable double-apply path: if the old PUT wins, the successor adopts it; if the successor's seal wins, the old PUT is rejected; if the generation changes before the old write is even sent, the old send is refused outright.; A valid successor seal must remain the conclusive rejection when an old I/O result returns after a generation bump, and the superseded runtime must not acknowledge, install, or publish it", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_plans_r1_findings.md#L22-L24", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r9_findings.md#closed-core-attacks"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0926", "topic": "The recommended fix for the CAS", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-314"], "canonical_claim": "The recommended fix for the CAS `fsck` seal-awareness gap requires a point read of the authority for every known namespace, traversal of the complete seal-descriptor history, exact-`GET` verification of every referenced seal, and treating missing/malformed/regressed/mismatched pointer-vs-seal state as fatal `unchecked`/corruption rather than clean.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r4_findings.md#L77-L83"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0927", "topic": "The \"natural black-box equivalence\" test oracle", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-315"], "canonical_claim": "The \"natural black-box equivalence\" test oracle requires a CA table to be byte-for-byte identical to a normal MergeTree table on the same data under active GC (stateless tests 04278/04279); divergence means a live blob was lost.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#4-oracle-patterns-strongest-weakest"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0928", "topic": "Under the one-pipeline transaction design, CA", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-395"], "canonical_claim": "Under the one-pipeline transaction design, CA manifest build, blob upload, and promote (remote I/O) deliberately run inside `MergeTreeData::Transaction::commit` under `lockParts()` (`MergeTreeData.cpp:8775/8792`); this is an accepted tradeoff per the spec's `Why There Is No Disk Precommit` rationale, not a regression, since moving remote I/O out of `data_parts` locking is a general commit-positioning/performance problem, not a CA-only correctness issue.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-16-cas-txn-one-pipeline.md#L129-L131"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0929", "topic": "CAS coordination state, including refs, mount", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B001-002"], "canonical_claim": "CAS coordination state, including refs, mount leases, and GC state, is stored in the object store; backend mutual exclusion relies on conditional PUT/DELETE (If-None-Match/If-Match) and startup probes fail closed.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L35-L42"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0930", "topic": "Every behavior-changing phase that changes deletion", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B001-030"], "canonical_claim": "Every behavior-changing phase that changes deletion semantics or GC safety must have a GREEN TLA+ model before implementation begins; negative controls must violate their named invariants.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/02-methodology.md#L103-L113"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0931", "topic": "The amended resurrection invariant permits reading", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B004-125"], "canonical_claim": "The amended resurrection invariant permits reading a condemned object only for verified copy-forward when a live committed manifest supplies `adoptEvidence`; it forbids blind PUT and `putIfAbsent` after a lost delete race.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-copy-forward-condemned-evidence.md#L60-L68"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0932", "topic": "The inequality `minack \u2264 K\u22121` prevents", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B005-027"], "canonical_claim": "The inequality `min_ack \u2264 K\u22121` prevents an entry condemned in pass K from graduating in that same pass, so the two-round condemnation pipeline follows from the round arithmetic.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#gc-round-protocol-one-pass"]}

